{"episode_id":"FE-2b7a9b-xf-source-flux-rigidity","mode":"prospective","source_window_base":"cec27ab2ab7e14ef1ef8e3849412abd6171bd574","source_window_head":"2b7a9b6d8525663ee114e18eaee06419d6286f8a","line":"xi_flow","atlas_territory":"RA-F3","classification":"viable-extension","source_paths":["research/xi_flow/findings/XF-034-super-mesoscopic-xi-counting-forces-geometric-gap-ramps-to-flatten.md","research/xi_flow/findings/XF-035-xi-counting-has-sharp-inverse-buffer-flux-variation-stability-threshold.md","research/xi_flow/findings/XF-036-translated-xi-counting-closes-the-borderline-flux-fold.md","research/xi_flow/findings/XF-037-borderline-flux-control-collapses-total-log-gap-variation.md"],"basis":"Xi-specific super-mesoscopic counting kills coherent geometric ramps, and at the borderline inverse-buffer flux scale translated counting forces not only uniform gap flattening but vanishing total logarithmic-gap variation. The live burden is now to derive that flux control from the Xi dynamics rather than postulate it."}
